# ocx `.bazelrc` — the committed, shipped rc.
#
# Every flag below was proven present on the pinned binary's two help surfaces
# (`bazel help build --long`, `bazel help startup_options`) at bazel 9.2.0
# before it was written here (BZL-FLAG-11). A flag absent from both surfaces
# and from the `--flag //... | grep 'Unrecognized option'` probe does not exist
# on this binary and hard-fails every command in the repository.
#
# The prefix on each line is load-bearing. A startup option on a `build` line
# is an `unrecognized option` error, not a warning.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Startup — parsed by the client, before the server exists.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Bazel server is a scheduler, not a compiler. Left to itself the JVM sizes
# its heap off total RAM; on the 32 GB development host that reserves multiples
# of what a workspace this size needs and starves rustc.
startup --host_jvm_args=-Xmx2g

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local resource envelope. `test` inherits every `build` option.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A RAM cap, not a speed choice — the same number `cargo build` already uses on
# this host, because it is the same constraint. CI runners are the opposite
# shape (few cores, no LSP, ephemeral) and get Bazel's own default back below.
build --jobs=12

# Never `/tmp`: on the development host that is a tmpfs, so a disk cache there
# is RAM and an hourly reaper deletes it mid-build.
#
# Nor anywhere inside the workspace, and the reason is mechanical rather than
# stylistic. Measured on 9.2.0: `%workspace%` is substituted in `import` and
# `try-import` statements ONLY — written into a flag value it is taken
# literally and Bazel creates a directory actually named `%workspace%`. And a
# relative `--disk_cache` resolves against the CLIENT WORKING DIRECTORY, not
# the workspace root, so `bazel` run from `crates/` silently forks a second,
# empty cache. A `~`-rooted absolute path is the only spelling in a committed
# rc that means one place from every directory — and one cache shared by the
# four fixed worktrees plus every agent worktree is the behaviour this repo
# wants anyway.
build --disk_cache=~/.cache/ocx/bazel-disk

# Disk-cache GC is deliberately OFF on this pin. Both
# `--experimental_disk_cache_gc_max_size` and
# `--experimental_disk_cache_gc_max_age` default to "0" (unbounded) even where
# supported, so this is unbounded growth and it is a known, accepted cost:
# collection runs only once the server has been idle for
# `--experimental_disk_cache_gc_idle_delay` (5m), which never happens on an
# ephemeral CI runner, so the flag would buy nothing on the lane that needs it
# and would expose the development host to the disk-cache TOCTOU in
# https://github.com/bazelbuild/bazel/issues/30836 — milestoned 9.3.0. Reopen
# when this workspace's pin crosses 9.3.0.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Remote cache — read-only everywhere this file reaches.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The scheme is explicit: both remote flags default to `grpcs` on a scheme-less
# URI, which silently talks the wrong protocol to an HTTP cache.
#
# `/v1` is the cache GENERATION, and it is a URI path prefix on purpose.
# `--remote_instance_name` is inert over an HTTP remote cache — Bazel's HTTP
# client never puts the instance name in the URL — so rotating a generation
# through that flag changes nothing at all. Do not re-add it here. The server
# side of the same exit is `--enable_ac_key_instance_mangling` on the
# bazel-remote unit; that is a server flag and no Bazel binary carries it.
build --remote_cache=https://bazel-cache.ocx.sh/v1

# Writes are granted per-lane, never by this file. A contributor, a fork PR and
# a non-main CI lane all read and none of them write.
build --remote_upload_local_results=false

# The default is 60s PER OPERATION. A single slow box with no failover, serving
# thousands of lookups per build, makes a build slower than no cache at all —
# green, with nothing noticing. A per-operation wait longer than the action it
# is trying to avoid is never a win.
build --remote_timeout=30s

# Stated rather than inherited so the worst case per operation is readable.
# This is not `--experimental_remote_cache_eviction_retries`, which stays at
# its default of 5 and is deliberately absent from this file.
build --remote_retries=2

# Download mode is per role (BZL-CACHE-11). Interactive machines keep the
# `toplevel` default and therefore set nothing here; a CI runner consumes no
# build output locally and gets `minimal` under `--config=ci`, below.
#
# The paired position BZL-CACHE-12 asks for, because `minimal` maximises
# exposure to the evicted-intermediate class: never key CI retry or alerting
# logic on exit code 39 — it was never observed reaching the caller. Match the
# log text instead: `lost inputs with digests:`, `Found transient remote cache
# error, retrying the build...`, `Lost inputs no longer available remotely:`,
# `lost input too many times (#21)`. And `--experimental_remote_cache_ttl`
# stays unset (3h default) until the server's real eviction window is known and
# shorter.
build:ci --remote_download_minimal

# Hand `--jobs` back to Bazel on the lane this file's local cap does not fit.
build:ci --jobs=auto

# Do NOT enable `--experimental_remote_cache_chunking`. It is present on 9.2.0
# and defaults false; turning it on re-keys CAS uploads and splits this
# workspace's cache population across two incompatible encodings for no
# measured win.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What deliberately is NOT in this file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# No credential of any kind, and no flag whose value is EXECUTED. Bazel
# resolves and spawns such a helper with the full client environment, before
# the sandbox — a read-only `bazel query //...` on a fresh clone is enough to
# run it, and upstream closed that as intended behaviour. The cache write
# credential is written by CI into an rc file under `$RUNNER_TEMP`, outside the
# workspace, and passed with `--bazelrc=`; CI's read credential arrives the same
# way. A developer's read credential is a `--remote_header` line in `~/.bazelrc`,
# never `.bazelrc.user` - host state must not need a per-checkout file, because
# this machine carries many worktrees and disposable ones. `task bazel:doctor`
# reds the `.bazelrc.user` spelling as `userrc-host-state`.
#
# No `--lockfile_mode=error` either. Unscoped in a committed base rc it turns
# every legitimate dependency edit into a failure a developer cannot
# self-serve; the freshness gate belongs on its own CI leg, as
# `bazel mod deps --lockfile_mode=error`, decided on the exit code.
#
# `--repo_contents_cache` is left at its default,
# `{--repository_cache}/contents` = `{--output_user_root}/cache/repos/v1/contents`
# — outside the workspace, shared across checkouts, and the one repository-
# shaped cache on this pin that does have real GC (age-only, 14d).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Personal overrides. MUST stay the last non-comment line: import position is
# the precedence contract, and an override imported anywhere else is silently
# defeated (BZL-FLAG-19).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
try-import %workspace%/.bazelrc.user
